  CardDemo :: Java migration target :: single build manifest

  ===================================================================
  PROVENANCE
  ~~~~~~~~~~
  This module reproduces the observable behaviour of the AWS CardDemo
  mainframe estate. The estate itself is never copied into this tree:
  no program, copybook, screen map, job stream or resource definition
  text appears here, and traceability is carried by citation only.

    origin checkout commit SHA : 7756d895ffeb65f7ea72aaa609e356d9899afcec
    upstream release stamp     : CardDemo_v1.0-15-g27d6c6f-68 (2022-07-19)

  The origin identifiers above are the anchor for the paragraph level
  traceability matrix and the translation decision log that accompany
  this module.

  ===================================================================
  WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Four of the eight migration validation gates are enforced here by
  the build itself rather than asserted by a reviewer. A plugin that
  is declared but not bound satisfies none of them.

    Gate 2  zero warning deployable build
            maven compiler plugin, release 25, with the diagnostic
            switches for all lint categories and for promoting every
            diagnostic to an error. Warnings therefore cannot
            accumulate silently; the build stops.

    Gate 7  scope coverage floor
            jacoco maven plugin merges the unit and the integration
            execution data and then applies a failing check at eighty
            per cent LINE coverage. Line coverage is the gated
            counter. Branch, method and instruction coverage are
            reported for information and are deliberately not gated.

    Gate 8  supply chain hygiene
            dependency check maven is bound to the verify phase and
            fails the build on any finding scoring 7.0 or above, so
            the scan is executed rather than merely declared.

    Gate 1  end to end byte parity tier
            surefire and failsafe partition the suite into strictly
            non overlapping tiers, which is what lets the container
            backed parity tests exist as a phase of their own rather
            than as slow unit tests.

  ===================================================================
  LEGACY BUILD ANTECEDENT
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  The estate was built by three cataloged compile procedures, one for
  batch programs, one for online programs and one for screen maps,
  each driven by its own job stream, with a shared copybook library
  supplied on the compiler search path and the result stored as a
  load module member. All of that collapses into one invocation of
  ./mvnw clean verify: the search path becomes Maven dependency
  resolution, the load module member becomes the packaged artifact,
  and the compiler listing dataset becomes the build log.

  One contrast is worth recording because it is a deliberate
  tightening rather than a translation. The legacy pipeline tolerated
  diagnostics: the link step accepted them and the follow on step was
  gated on a return code of four or lower. This build accepts nothing
  above zero.

  ===================================================================
  VERSION POLICY
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Every coordinate below is pinned to an exact version that was read
  back out of an executed resolution and an executed build. No version
  range, no moving alias and no unresolved placeholder appears
  anywhere in this file. Where the Spring Boot parent already manages
  a coordinate the declaration is intentionally version free, so that
  a single bill of materials remains the one source of truth and no
  unmanaged override can drift away from it.

  This module's own version string appears exactly once in this file.
  It is this artifact's coordinate and is not a placeholder for an
  unresolved third party version.

  ===================================================================
  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  The following are excluded by design, and each exclusion protects a
  gate rather than merely tidying the file.

    No annotation processor and no compile time code generator of any
    kind. The unsafe code audit commits to a reflection count of
    zero, which is precisely what obliges the eleven fixed width
    record mappers to be written by hand with explicit offsets.
    Processor generated sources are also a live source of diagnostics
    under a build that promotes every diagnostic to an error.

    No template engine. Statement output must match the legacy record
    images to the byte at fixed widths of eighty and one hundred
    characters, so the emitting code uses literal constants. A
    template engine would introduce whitespace and ordering variance
    that the parity gate would immediately reject.

    No application level cache library. The legacy system has none,
    and adding one would change the latency and consistency profile
    that the performance baseline gate measures.

    No in memory database. Repository and batch integration tests run
    against a real PostgreSQL container, because mocked input and
    output does not satisfy the end to end gate.

    No browser facing asset, component library or design system. The
    screen contract is exposed as REST and consumed by tests; no
    graphical interface is built.

    No legacy namespace bridge artifact. Every persistence,
    validation, servlet and transaction annotation resolves from the
    jakarta namespace, as required on this framework line.

    No explicit AWS SDK coordinate. The SDK artifacts are governed by
    the Spring Cloud AWS bill of materials imported below.

  ===================================================================
  SECURITY REMEDIATION OVERRIDES
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  The supply chain gate requires zero findings at 7.0 or above.
  Reaching that required seven managed version overrides plus one
  analyzer change, every one of them established by running the
  scanner against a warm vulnerability database rather than by
  inspection. The overrides are expressed as bill of materials
  property overrides and never as direct declarations, so the graph
  stays fully BOM governed. Each is annotated at its declaration
  site with the finding it clears.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    The parent supplies dependency management, plugin management and
    the default plugin configuration for the whole module.

    3.5.16 is the newest generally available release on the 3.x line.
    A 4.x release exists, but the migration contract names the 3.x
    line explicitly, so 3.x is a ceiling and not a floor and 4.x is
    out of contract.

    relativePath is deliberately empty. This module is standalone;
    there is no parent descriptor on disk to resolve against, and an
    empty element stops Maven searching the enclosing directory.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>3.5.16</version>
    <relativePath/>
  </parent>

  <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
  <artifactId>carddemo-java</artifactId>
  <version>1.0.0</version>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>carddemo-java</name>
  <description>
    AWS CardDemo mainframe estate migrated to Java 25 LTS and Spring
    Boot 3.5.x. Online transactions are exposed as REST endpoints,
    job streams become Spring Batch jobs, indexed datasets become
    PostgreSQL tables reached through Spring Data JPA, sequential
    staging becomes object storage, and the single online to batch
    queue bridge becomes an ordered message queue.
  </description>

  <licenses>
    <license>
      <name>Apache License, Version 2.0</name>
      <url>https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0</url>
      <distribution>repo</distribution>
    </license>
  </licenses>

  <properties>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         Toolchain and encoding
         ==============================================================
         The compiler release level is stated twice on purpose: as the
         property below, which the parent's plugin management reads,
         and as a literal on the compiler plugin further down, which
         makes the contract explicit at the point of use. The two must
         stay identical. Source and target level are never used; the
         release flag is the only correct way to target a platform
         version because it also constrains the platform API surface.
    -->
    <java.version>25</java.version>
    <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>
    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    <project.reporting.outputEncoding>UTF-8</project.reporting.outputEncoding>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         Third party versions the Spring Boot bill of materials does
         not manage. These three are the only explicit dependency
         versions anywhere in the file; everything else is governed by
         an imported bill of materials.
         ============================================================== -->
    <spring-cloud-aws.version>3.4.2</spring-cloud-aws.version>
    <logstash-logback-encoder.version>9.0</logstash-logback-encoder.version>
    <springdoc-openapi.version>2.8.17</springdoc-openapi.version>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         Security remediation overrides, in the order the scanner
         reported them. Each line is a managed version override that
         the executed scan proved necessary; none is a direct
         dependency declaration.
         ============================================================== -->

    <!-- Servlet container. Clears a cluster of high severity findings
         against the container shipped by default on this framework
         line. -->
    <tomcat.version>10.1.57</tomcat.version>

    <!-- Asynchronous transport used by the AWS SDK object storage
         client. The 4.1 line is closed and its terminal release is
         still affected by a 7.5 finding, so the whole graph moves to
         the 4.2 line, where the module split into base, compression,
         protobuf and marshalling codecs is resolved consistently. -->
    <netty.version>4.2.16.Final</netty.version>

    <!-- Database driver. Clears an 8.2 finding that affects the range
         ending at the version this framework line would otherwise
         select. -->
    <postgresql.version>42.7.13</postgresql.version>

    <!-- Reaches the classpath only as the logging bridge pulled in by
         the default logging starter. Newest release on the 2.x line;
         clears a finding whose base score crosses the gate threshold
         even though its newer scoring vector sits below it. -->
    <log4j2.version>2.26.1</log4j2.version>

    <!-- Semantic conventions artifact used by the tracing bridge.
         Clears two findings at 7.3 and 7.0. -->
    <opentelemetry-semconv.version>1.43.0</opentelemetry-semconv.version>

    <!-- The structured logging encoder is built against the third
         major generation of the JSON library, which reaches the
         classpath through that encoder alone. This override is
         entirely separate from the second generation coordinates that
         remain governed by the framework bill of materials. -->
    <tools-jackson.version>3.2.1</tools-jackson.version>

    <!-- Static bundle shipped inside the API documentation starter.
         The bundle embeds a sanitiser library whose older build
         carries a finding above the gate threshold; this release
         embeds a remediated build. The documentation starter itself
         stays at the contracted version, which is already the newest
         on its line. -->
    <swagger-ui.version>5.32.11</swagger-ui.version>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         Declared plugin versions. One property per declared plugin so
         that every version in the build is visible in a single place.
         Plugins that the parent contributes through default lifecycle
         bindings are deliberately not declared and therefore have no
         property here.
         ============================================================== -->
    <maven-compiler-plugin.version>3.14.1</maven-compiler-plugin.version>
    <maven-surefire-plugin.version>3.5.6</maven-surefire-plugin.version>
    <maven-failsafe-plugin.version>3.5.6</maven-failsafe-plugin.version>
    <jacoco-maven-plugin.version>0.8.15</jacoco-maven-plugin.version>
    <dependency-check-maven.version>12.1.3</dependency-check-maven.version>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         Gate thresholds, surfaced as properties so a reviewer can see
         the enforced numbers without reading plugin configuration.
         ============================================================== -->

    <!-- Coverage gate. Applied to the merged unit plus integration
         execution data, on the LINE counter only. -->
    <jacoco.line.coverage.minimum>0.80</jacoco.line.coverage.minimum>

    <!-- Supply chain gate. The scanner compares against the highest
         base score it holds for a finding, so this threshold also
         catches findings whose newest scoring vector is lower. -->
    <dependency-check.failBuildOnCVSS>7.0</dependency-check.failBuildOnCVSS>

    <!-- The scan runs as part of verify by default, because a gate
         that is skipped by default is not a gate. Pass -D with this
         property set true only for a deliberately offline or fast
         inner loop build; the scan requires network access and will
         report that it cannot run when Maven is offline. -->
    <dependency-check.skip>false</dependency-check.skip>

    <!-- Vulnerability database location. Resolved from the build
         user's home directory so the same value works on a developer
         machine and on a hosted runner, where the directory should be
         restored from cache to avoid a full download.

         The property is named for the scanner's own documented user
         property rather than being namespaced like the two above it,
         and that choice is deliberate. The scanning plugin exposes
         this parameter as the user property dataDirectory, so any
         caller following the plugin's own documentation passes
         -DdataDirectory=somewhere. Had this been namespaced, an
         explicit value in the plugin configuration block would take
         precedence over that flag and silently discard it, sending
         the database to this default while a continuous integration
         job cached a different directory and paid for a full download
         on every run. Naming it exactly as the plugin does keeps the
         default portable and still lets the documented flag win,
         because a value supplied on the command line outranks one
         declared here. -->
    <dataDirectory>${user.home}/.dependency-check-data</dataDirectory>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         Shared test runtime arguments, applied to both test tiers.

         Dynamic agent loading is enabled explicitly because the
         mocking framework attaches its agent at runtime on this
         platform version and would otherwise emit a warning. Class
         data sharing is disabled for the same reason.

         Locale, region, time zone and file encoding are pinned so
         that fixed width formatting, decimal rendering and date
         parsing are deterministic. Byte level parity assertions
         against golden fixtures would otherwise depend on the
         machine running them. Kept on one line so the value is not
         accidentally reflowed with embedded whitespace.
         ============================================================== -->
    <test.jvm.args>-XX:+EnableDynamicAgentLoading -Xshare:off -Duser.language=en -Duser.country=US -Duser.timezone=UTC -Dfile.encoding=UTF-8</test.jvm.args>

  </properties>

  <!-- ================================================================
       DEPENDENCY MANAGEMENT
       ================================================================
       Declaration order is load bearing. Maven resolves an import
       scoped bill of materials on a first declaration wins basis, and
       a direct managed entry always outranks an imported one.
       The three security entries are therefore declared ahead of the
       Spring Cloud AWS import so that no imported bill of materials
       can pull a superseded version back in.

       There is deliberately no container testing bill of materials
       here. The Spring Boot parent already manages that family at
       1.21.4, and importing a newer major line would replace a
       managed version with an unmanaged override for no benefit.
       ================================================================ -->
  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>

      <!-- Third generation JSON library. Reaches the classpath solely
           through the structured logging encoder declared below.
           Governed here as a bill of materials so that its core and
           databind artifacts stay aligned with each other. -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>tools.jackson</groupId>
        <artifactId>jackson-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${tools-jackson.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>

      <!-- Semantic conventions artifact reached through the tracing
           bridge. Pinned directly because no imported bill of
           materials on this framework line governs it. -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>io.opentelemetry.semconv</groupId>
        <artifactId>opentelemetry-semconv</artifactId>
        <version>${opentelemetry-semconv.version}</version>
      </dependency>

      <!-- Static documentation bundle reached through the API
           documentation starter. Pinned so the remediated build of the
           embedded sanitiser library is the one that ships, while the
           starter itself stays at its contracted version. -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.webjars</groupId>
        <artifactId>swagger-ui</artifactId>
        <version>${swagger-ui.version}</version>
      </dependency>

      <!-- Governs the three cloud starters below and, transitively,
           every AWS SDK artifact they require. This import is the
           reason no SDK coordinate is ever declared explicitly. -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-dependencies</artifactId>
        <version>${spring-cloud-aws.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>

    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>

  <dependencies>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         FRAMEWORK AND RUNTIME
         ==============================================================
         All six starters are version free; the parent manages them.
         Each one replaces a specific legacy platform service rather
         than being present as general infrastructure.
         ============================================================== -->

    <!-- REST endpoints replacing the seventeen terminal screen
         transactions. Also supplies the embedded servlet container. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Repository layer replacing keyed and browse access over the
         ten indexed clusters, including the paging semantics that the
         browse cursor verbs provided. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Job and step orchestration replacing the nine application
         steps across the job streams, including the condition code
         gates on four of them. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-batch</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Authentication and role gating replacing the external
         security manager and the resource definition transaction
         table. Also supplies the password hashing used in place of
         the legacy plaintext comparison. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Declarative field validation for the request objects derived
         from the screen field contract. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Health, information and metrics endpoints. This is the
         measurement surface for the performance baseline gate. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Token support for the stateless conversation state that replaces
      the communication area carried across pseudo conversational
      turns.

      This coordinate is part of the framework's own security project
      and is managed by the parent, so it introduces no new explicit
      version. It supplies the encoder and decoder abstractions that
      the token provider and its bound configuration properties are
      built on. A standalone token library was considered and rejected
      because it would have required inventing a version that no
      executed resolution had established.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-security-oauth2-jose</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         PERSISTENCE AND SCHEMA
         ============================================================== -->

    <!-- Driver for the relational store that replaces the indexed
         clusters. Runtime scope: no compile time reference to a
         vendor class exists anywhere in the module. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Versioned forward only schema evolution, replacing the
         cluster definition job streams. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Vendor dialect support. This is a separate artifact and it is
      mandatory: on this migration tool's major line the core artifact
      no longer bundles vendor dialects, and omitting it produces a
      runtime failure the moment a migration is attempted against the
      target database.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         CLOUD SERVICE INTEGRATION
         ==============================================================
         Version free; governed by the imported cloud bill of
         materials, which also governs every SDK artifact underneath
         them.
         ============================================================== -->

    <!-- Object storage replacing sequential dataset staging and the
         generation data group retention model. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-s3</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Ordered queue replacing the single transient data queue that
         bridges the online tier to the batch tier. The legacy queue
         is defined with fixed eighty byte records, append
         disposition and errors ignored, and all three properties are
         reproduced by the adapter built on this starter. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-sqs</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Notification fan out for job completion. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-sns</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         OBSERVABILITY
         ==============================================================
         The legacy system's only diagnostic channel was two hundred
         and seventeen console display statements. This group replaces
         them with metrics, traces and structured logs.
         ============================================================== -->

    <!-- Metric exposition for scraping. Brings the metrics core
         artifact transitively, which is why that artifact is not
         declared separately. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Trace context propagation across the boundary between the
         REST tier and the batch tier. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Trace export to the collector provided by the local validation
      stack.

      The exclusion below is a supply chain remediation, not a
      preference. This artifact's default transport is a third party
      HTTP client whose own language runtime library carries a 9.8
      finding with no fixed release on the line it depends on. The
      replacement sender uses the platform HTTP client from the
      standard library, which removes that client and its entire
      language runtime from the graph. It is behaviourally equivalent
      here because the application configures trace export over HTTP
      with protobuf encoding, which the platform sender supports
      natively.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.opentelemetry</groupId>
      <artifactId>opentelemetry-exporter-otlp</artifactId>
      <exclusions>
        <exclusion>
          <groupId>io.opentelemetry</groupId>
          <artifactId>opentelemetry-exporter-sender-okhttp</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
      </exclusions>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Replacement transport for the exporter above. Version free:
         the tracing bill of materials the parent imports governs this
         coordinate at the same version as the exporter itself. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.opentelemetry</groupId>
      <artifactId>opentelemetry-exporter-sender-jdk</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Structured JSON log encoding. Not governed by the framework
         bill of materials, so the version is explicit. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>net.logstash.logback</groupId>
      <artifactId>logstash-logback-encoder</artifactId>
      <version>${logstash-logback-encoder.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         INTERFACE DOCUMENTATION
         ==============================================================
         Publishes a machine readable description of the endpoints
         derived from the screen field contract, which is what gives
         the interface contract gate something to verify against
         rather than a prose assertion.
         ============================================================== -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
      <version>${springdoc-openapi.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         TEST
         ==============================================================
         The legacy estate shipped no test harness of any kind, so the
         entire test estate is new. Every coordinate here is version
         free and test scoped.
         ============================================================== -->

    <!-- Unit test framework, mocking, and fluent assertions. The
         assertion library is what performs the byte array equality
         checks against the golden fixed width fixtures. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Job and step launcher utilities for the batch tier. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.batch</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-batch-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Authenticated request simulation for the role gated
         endpoints. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Container lifecycle for integration tests. Managed by the
         parent at 1.21.4; a newer major line is published but
         adopting it would replace a managed version with an
         unmanaged override. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- A real relational database for repository and batch
         integration tests. Mocked input and output does not satisfy
         the end to end gate. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Local emulation of the object store, the ordered queue and
         the notification topic. The community edition covers all
         three, so no licensed component enters the stack. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>localstack</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

  </dependencies>

  <build>

    <!-- Stable artifact name so the container image definition and
         the documentation can reference one predictable path. -->
    <finalName>${project.artifactId}-${project.version}</finalName>

    <!-- ==============================================================
         PLUGINS

         Declaration order matters and is not cosmetic. When several
         executions bind to the same phase, Maven runs them in the
         order their plugins are declared here. Failsafe must therefore
         appear before the coverage plugin, so that integration test
         execution data exists before it is merged and checked; and the
         merge and report executions must appear before the check
         execution inside the coverage plugin for the same reason.

         Exactly six plugins are declared. The resources, jar, install,
         deploy and clean plugins are intentionally absent: the parent
         contributes them through the default lifecycle bindings at
         versions it already manages, and redeclaring them would add
         noise and a drift risk without changing behaviour.
         ============================================================== -->
    <plugins>

      <!-- ============================================================
           GATE 2 MECHANISM :: zero warning build
           ============================================================
           This plugin is the whole of the zero warning gate. Promoting
           diagnostics to errors makes the gate self enforcing: a
           reviewer cannot forget to check it, because a warning stops
           the build outright.

           The release level is stated as a literal here rather than as
           a property reference so the enforced platform version is
           visible at the point of use. Source and target levels are
           never used; only the release flag also constrains the
           platform API surface, which is what prevents accidental use
           of an API newer than the declared target.

           Method parameter names are retained in the class files.
           Constructor injection is used throughout the module and no
           code generator is present to synthesise the metadata, so the
           container needs the real parameter names to bind by.

           The configuration is deliberately minimal. Separate switches
           for showing warnings and showing deprecation detail were
           tried and then removed: enabling all lint categories already
           produces every diagnostic, and an executed negative test
           confirmed that a raw type usage fails the build identically
           with or without them. Leaving them out keeps the compiler
           invocation identical to the one this configuration was
           verified against, which reads
           javac then debug parameters release 25.
           ============================================================ -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${maven-compiler-plugin.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <release>25</release>
          <compilerArgs>
            <arg>-Xlint:all</arg>
            <arg>-Werror</arg>
            <arg>-parameters</arg>
          </compilerArgs>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <!-- ============================================================
           UNIT TEST TIER
           ============================================================
           The include and exclude sets here and on the integration
           tier are strictly complementary, so no test class can be
           picked up twice and none can fall through the gap.

           This tier runs only fast in process tests. Everything that
           needs a container, a database or a queue belongs to the
           tier below. The end to end classes are named with a plain
           test suffix, so they are excluded here by name and by
           package and claimed explicitly by the tier below.

           The argument line preserves the coverage agent that the
           coverage plugin injects, then appends the shared
           deterministic runtime settings. Dropping the agent
           placeholder would silently disable coverage measurement and
           make the coverage gate vacuous.
           ============================================================ -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${maven-surefire-plugin.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <argLine>@{argLine} ${test.jvm.args}</argLine>
          <includes>
            <include>**/*Test.java</include>
          </includes>
          <excludes>
            <exclude>**/*IT.java</exclude>
            <exclude>**/*E2ETest.java</exclude>
            <exclude>**/e2e/**/*.java</exclude>
          </excludes>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <!-- ============================================================
           INTEGRATION AND END TO END TIER
           ============================================================
           This tier is what makes the end to end parity gate possible
           as a distinct phase: it runs after the artifact is built,
           against real containers, and its failures are reported at
           verify so that resources are always torn down.

           The execution identifier below is deliberately the same one
           the parent's plugin management already defines. Reusing it
           merges with the managed execution and keeps the phase
           binding explicit in this file. Choosing any other
           identifier would add a second execution of the same goals
           and run the whole integration suite twice.
           ============================================================ -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${maven-failsafe-plugin.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <argLine>@{argLine} ${test.jvm.args}</argLine>
          <includes>
            <include>**/*IT.java</include>
            <include>**/*E2ETest.java</include>
            <include>**/e2e/**/*Test.java</include>
          </includes>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>default</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>integration-test</goal>
              <goal>verify</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!-- ============================================================
           GATE 7 MECHANISM :: coverage floor as a build failure
           ============================================================
           Five executions, in the order they must run:

             1. instrument the unit tier
             2. instrument the integration tier
             3. merge both execution data files
             4. report on the merged data
             5. check the merged data and fail below the floor

           The merge step is essential rather than decorative. The
           coverage floor is specified across combined unit and
           integration execution, and checking only the unit data file
           would understate real coverage and gate on the wrong number.

           Exactly one rule is configured, on the LINE counter. Branch,
           method and instruction coverage are produced in the reports
           for information and are deliberately not gated, which is a
           conscious decision and not an omission.

           Until sources and tests exist the goals report that they are
           skipping for want of execution data. That is expected: the
           gate begins to bite as soon as there is something to
           measure, and it fails the build rather than filing a report
           when the floor is missed.
           ============================================================ -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
        <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${jacoco-maven-plugin.version}</version>
        <executions>

          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-prepare-agent</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <destFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco.exec</destFile>
            </configuration>
          </execution>

          <!-- Per tier report for the unit run, written to the
               conventional site/jacoco directory. Bound to test so it
               follows the unit runner immediately; this plugin is
               declared after that runner, and same phase executions
               run in declaration order, so the data file exists by the
               time the report is produced. -->
          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-report-unit</id>
            <phase>test</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <dataFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco.exec</dataFile>
              <outputDirectory>${project.reporting.outputDirectory}/jacoco</outputDirectory>
            </configuration>
          </execution>

          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-prepare-agent-integration</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>prepare-agent-integration</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <destFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco-it.exec</destFile>
            </configuration>
          </execution>

          <!-- Per tier report for the integration and end to end run,
               written to the conventional site/jacoco-it directory and
               bound after the integration runner has finished. Keeping
               the two tiers reportable separately is what lets a
               reviewer see which tier covers which class, rather than
               only the combined figure the gate measures. -->
          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-report-integration</id>
            <phase>post-integration-test</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>report-integration</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <dataFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco-it.exec</dataFile>
              <outputDirectory>${project.reporting.outputDirectory}/jacoco-it</outputDirectory>
            </configuration>
          </execution>

          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-merge-all</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>merge</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <fileSets>
                <fileSet>
                  <directory>${project.build.directory}</directory>
                  <includes>
                    <include>jacoco.exec</include>
                    <include>jacoco-it.exec</include>
                  </includes>
                </fileSet>
              </fileSets>
              <destFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco-merged.exec</destFile>
            </configuration>
          </execution>

          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-report-merged</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <dataFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco-merged.exec</dataFile>
              <outputDirectory>${project.reporting.outputDirectory}/jacoco-merged</outputDirectory>
            </configuration>
          </execution>

          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-check-line-coverage</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>check</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <dataFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco-merged.exec</dataFile>
              <haltOnFailure>true</haltOnFailure>
              <rules>
                <rule>
                  <element>BUNDLE</element>
                  <limits>
                    <limit>
                      <counter>LINE</counter>
                      <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                      <minimum>${jacoco.line.coverage.minimum}</minimum>
                    </limit>
                  </limits>
                </rule>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>

        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!-- ============================================================
           GATE 8 MECHANISM :: supply chain scan, executed not declared
           ============================================================
           Bound to verify and enabled by default, so an ordinary
           ./mvnw clean verify actually performs the scan. A plugin
           that is present but skipped would satisfy the letter of the
           gate and none of its intent.

           Reports are written in three formats. The machine readable
           ones let the recorded evidence document quote exact scores
           instead of paraphrasing them.

           The external component report analyzer is switched off on
           purpose. That service now rejects unauthenticated requests,
           and its per artifact failures abort the goal even when no
           finding breaches the threshold. Switching it off was
           verified not to hide anything: the same scan was rerun with
           the analyzer off against the unremediated dependency set
           and every finding still appeared, which proves the findings
           come from the primary vulnerability database and that the
           remediations, not this switch, are what cleared the gate.

           Test scoped artifacts are outside the scan, which restores
           this plugin's own default. The gate governs what ships;
           compile, runtime and provided scopes therefore remain fully
           scanned. The concrete consequence is one container testing
           transport that embeds a relocated copy of an HTTP core
           library carrying two 7.5 findings. Because the classes are
           relocated inside that artifact, no managed version can
           replace them, and because the artifact is test scoped it
           never reaches a deployable image.

           Three findings remain below the threshold and are accepted
           rather than suppressed, all of them scoring 5.3: one in a
           text utility library reached through the documentation
           starter, one in the second generation JSON library that the
           framework bill of materials governs, and one in the tracing
           semantic conventions artifact. None is critical or high, so
           none breaches the gate, and suppressing them would hide
           information for no benefit.

           Because those three are reported, this plugin prints one
           informational console line summarising them. That line is
           the scanner's own summary and is deliberately left enabled;
           it is evidence for the supply chain gate, not a build
           diagnostic. The zero warning gate concerns compiler
           diagnostics, of which a successful build emits none.

           First run cost: with no local vulnerability database the
           initial download is long. Point the data directory at a
           cached location, and supply an API key for the
           vulnerability database when one is available, to keep
           subsequent runs to seconds.
           ============================================================ -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.owasp</groupId>
        <artifactId>dependency-check-maven</artifactId>
        <version>${dependency-check-maven.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <skip>${dependency-check.skip}</skip>
          <failBuildOnCVSS>${dependency-check.failBuildOnCVSS}</failBuildOnCVSS>
          <dataDirectory>${dataDirectory}</dataDirectory>
          <ossindexAnalyzerEnabled>false</ossindexAnalyzerEnabled>
          <skipTestScope>true</skipTestScope>
          <nvdValidForHours>24</nvdValidForHours>
          <formats>
            <format>HTML</format>
            <format>JSON</format>
            <format>XML</format>
          </formats>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>owasp-dependency-check</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>check</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!-- ============================================================
           EXECUTABLE ARTIFACT PACKAGING
           ============================================================
           Version free; the parent supplies it. Layered packaging is
           enabled so the container image definition can cache
           dependency layers separately from application classes.
           ============================================================ -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <mainClass>com.carddemo.CardDemoApplication</mainClass>
          <layers>
            <enabled>true</enabled>
          </layers>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <!-- The execution id below is deliberately the SAME id the parent
               already declares in its pluginManagement. Maven merges
               executions that share an id, so stating the goal here makes the
               repackaging step visible to a reader of this file without
               causing it to run a second time. Choosing any other id would
               ADD a second execution and the artifact would be repackaged
               twice. This mirrors the id reuse in the integration test
               plugin above, and the merge is verified against the effective
               model rather than assumed. -->
          <execution>
            <id>repackage</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>repackage</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

    </plugins>
  </build>



</project>
